{"causes": ["Sociology's emergence as a distinct field was influenced by the works of Émile Durkheim, who studied the division of labor and its effects on social solidarity. The French Revolution's emphasis on liberty, equality, and fraternity also contributed to the development of sociological thought. Additionally, the rise of urbanization in 19th-century Europe led to increased interest in understanding the dynamics of cities and their inhabitants.", "The publication of Auguste Comte's 'Positive Philosophy' in 1848 marked a significant milestone in the history of sociology. Comte's positivist approach emphasized the scientific study of social phenomena, laying the groundwork for future sociological research. The Russian Revolution's impact on social theory also played a role in shaping sociology as we know it today.", "The rise of modernity and the growth of cities led to increased concern about social inequality and the need for reform. Sociologists like Georg Simmel and Max Weber explored the relationships between urbanization, culture, and social change, further solidifying the discipline's focus on understanding human societies."], "effects": ["Sociology has had a profound impact on our understanding of modern society, influencing fields such as psychology, politics, economics, and culture. The discipline has also shaped policy debates and public discourse around issues like social inequality, education, and healthcare.", "The development of sociological theories has led to the creation of new social policies, such as affirmative action programs and social welfare initiatives. Sociologists have also played a crucial role in understanding the impact of globalization on local communities and economies.", "Sociology's focus on collective behavior and social movements has helped to inform our understanding of protest, activism, and social change. The discipline has also contributed to the development of more nuanced theories about power, privilege, and identity."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "Émile Durkheim", "sourceName": "Émile Durkheim", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "Sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "Durkheim's work on the division of labor and its effects on social solidarity"}, {"sourceSlug": "Auguste Comte", "sourceName": "Auguste Comte", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "Sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "Comte's 'Positive Philosophy' marked a significant milestone in the history of sociology"}, {"sourceSlug": "Georg Simmel", "sourceName": "Georg Simmel", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "Sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "Simmel's work on urbanization and culture influenced the development of sociological thought"}, {"sourceSlug": "Max Weber", "sourceName": "Max Weber", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "Sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "Weber's theories on bureaucracy and social change further solidified sociology's focus on understanding human societies"}, {"sourceSlug": "French Revolution", "sourceName": "French Revolution", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "Sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "The Revolution's emphasis on liberty, equality, and fraternity contributed to the development of sociological thought"}, {"sourceSlug": "Urbanization", "sourceName": "Urbanization", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "Sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "The rise of urbanization in 19th-century Europe led to increased interest in understanding the dynamics of cities and their inhabitants"}, {"sourceSlug": "Russian Revolution", "sourceName": "Russian Revolution", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "Sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "The Revolution's impact on social theory also played a role in shaping sociology as we know it today"}, {"sourceSlug": "Auguste Comte", "sourceName": "Auguste Comte", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "Sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "Comte's positivist approach emphasized the scientific study of social phenomena, laying the groundwork for future sociological research"}], "places": [{"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Berlin, Germany", "role": "Influence"}, {"name": "Vienna, Austria", "role": "Center of Sociological Thought"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: sociology", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "sociology"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q21201", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sociology"], "tags": ["academic major", "Scientific Paradigms", "scientific-paradigm"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "sociology is remembered as a landmark idea of the Classical era, referenced in 201 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced intellectual traditions and cultural development.", "summary": "Sociology is the scientific study of human social life, groups, organisations, and societies — the academic discipline that emerged in the 19th century as a systematic attempt to apply scientific methods to the study of the social world. The term was coined by Auguste Comte (1798–1857), who proposed sociology as the queen of the sciences — the discipline that would synthesise all knowledge about human affairs and provide a scientific basis for social reform and the organisation of modern industrial society. Comte's positivism — the programme of applying empirical scientific method to social phenomena — laid the philosophical foundation for sociology's ambition to be a science of society analogous to the natural sciences.

The classical period of sociological theory (c. 1840–1920) produced the discipline's foundational frameworks: Émile Durkheim (1858–1917) demonstrated the power of sociological explanation through his studies of suicide, religion, and the division of labour — arguing that social facts (such as suicide rates) could not be reduced to individual psychology and required specifically sociological explanation. Max Weber (1864–1920) developed interpretive sociology and the concepts of social action, bureaucracy, rationalisation, and the Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism — his thesis that Protestant Calvinist culture promoted capitalist economic attitudes becoming one of the most debated propositions in modern social science. Karl Marx's historical materialism — the theory that the material organisation of production determines social structure and ideology — was another founding framework, though Marx himself was not primarily a sociologist.

In the 20th century, sociology diversified into numerous sub-fields and theoretical schools: structural functionalism (Talcott Parsons), conflict theory, symbolic interactionism, ethnomethodology, feminist sociology, critical theory (the Frankfurt School), and postmodern sociology (Bourdieu, Giddens, Foucault as interlocutors). Sociological methods expanded from survey research and statistical analysis to ethnography, interviews, discourse analysis, and computational social science, making sociology one of the most methodologically diverse disciplines in the social sciences.", "subjects": ["Social Science", "Academic Disciplines", "Modern Era", "Sociology", "Social Theory", "Intellectual History", "19th Century", "Social Science History"], "frameworks": ["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS", "CAUSE_AND_EFFECT", "WORLD_SYSTEMS"], "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Sociology has had a profound impact on our understanding of modern society, influencing fields such as psychology, politics, economics, and culture. The discipline has also shaped policy debates and public discourse around issues like social inequality, education, and healthcare.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-05-17T03:33:09Z", "editorId": "ollama/llama3.2:3b·local·manasa151-MS-7C56", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Social science that studies human society and its development", "newValue": "Sociology is a social science that studies human society and its development, focusing on the interactions between individuals, groups, and societies. Founded in 19th-century Europe, sociology emerged as a distinct discipline with Émile Durkheim's work on the division of labor and Marcel Mauss's study of gift economies. The field has since expanded to encompass global perspectives, interdisciplinary approaches, and diverse theoretical frameworks. Sociology seeks to understand social structures, institutions, and processes that shape human experience, influencing fields such as psychology, politics, economics, and culture. By examining power dynamics, social inequality, and collective behavior, sociologists aim to improve social policies, promote social justice, and foster a more informed and empathetic public. The discipline has played a crucial role in shaping our understanding of modern society, from the rise of urbanization and industrialization to the impact of globalization and technological change."}, {"timestamp": "2026-05-17T03:33:09Z", "editorId": "ollama/llama3.2:3b·local·manasa151-MS-7C56", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["The Industrial Revolution and the social dislocations of 19th-century urbanisation, class conflict, and the disruption of traditional communities — the 'social question' of how to understand and manage the new industrial society — created the intellectual demand for a scientific study of social life that sociology was designed to meet.", "The Enlightenment tradition of applying reason and empirical method to understanding the natural world — and its extension to social institutions by thinkers like Montesquieu, Condorcet, and Saint-Simon — provided the intellectual precedents for Comte's positivist programme of a science of society.", "The institutional development of the modern university in the 19th century — and the creation of academic disciplines with their own journals, professional associations, and curricula — provided the institutional infrastructure within which sociology could establish itself as an academic field."], "newValue": ["Sociology's emergence as a distinct field was influenced by the works of Émile Durkheim, who studied the division of labor and its effects on social solidarity. The French Revolution's emphasis on liberty, equality, and fraternity also contributed to the development of sociological thought. Additionally, the rise of urbanization in 19th-century Europe led to increased interest in understanding the dynamics of cities and their inhabitants.", "The publication of Auguste Comte's 'Positive Philosophy' in 1848 marked a significant milestone in the history of sociology. Comte's positivist approach emphasized the scientific study of social phenomena, laying the groundwork for future sociological research. The Russian Revolution's impact on social theory also played a role in shaping sociology as we know it today.", "The rise of modernity and the growth of cities led to increased concern about social inequality and the need for reform. Sociologists like Georg Simmel and Max Weber explored the relationships between urbanization, culture, and social change, further solidifying the discipline's focus on understanding human societies."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-05-17T03:33:09Z", "editorId": "ollama/llama3.2:3b·local·manasa151-MS-7C56", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Sociology's development of theoretical frameworks — Durkheim's social facts, Weber's rationalism and bureaucracy, Marx's class analysis — provided the conceptual tools that shaped how modern societies understand themselves, their institutions, and their problems; these frameworks influenced policy, law, education, and political thought across the 20th century.", "The sociological concept of social structure — the idea that human behaviour is shaped by patterns of social organisation, norms, institutions, and inequalities that exist independently of individual will — has become a fundamental presupposition of modern social science, politics, and policy, shaping how modern states approach poverty, crime, education, and health.", "Sociology's sub-disciplines — medical sociology, criminology, urban sociology, the sociology of education, organisational sociology, gender and race studies — have produced empirical research and theoretical frameworks that directly inform professional practice, policy-making, and social reform in every domain of modern life."], "newValue": ["Sociology has had a profound impact on our understanding of modern society, influencing fields such as psychology, politics, economics, and culture. The discipline has also shaped policy debates and public discourse around issues like social inequality, education, and healthcare.", "The development of sociological theories has led to the creation of new social policies, such as affirmative action programs and social welfare initiatives. Sociologists have also played a crucial role in understanding the impact of globalization on local communities and economies.", "Sociology's focus on collective behavior and social movements has helped to inform our understanding of protest, activism, and social change. The discipline has also contributed to the development of more nuanced theories about power, privilege, and identity."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-05-17T03:33:09Z", "editorId": "ollama/llama3.2:3b·local·manasa151-MS-7C56", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "sociology", "sourceName": "Sociology", "verb": "FOUNDED_BY", "targetSlug": "auguste-comte", "targetName": "Auguste Comte", "context": "Comte coined the term 'sociology' and proposed it as the capstone science of human affairs — his positivist programme of applying scientific method to social life laid the philosophical foundation for the discipline."}, {"sourceSlug": "emile-durkheim", "sourceName": "Émile Durkheim", "verb": "SHAPES", "targetSlug": "sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "Durkheim's empirical studies of suicide, religion, and the division of labour established the canonical methods and central concepts of academic sociology — his social facts approach defined sociology's explanatory distinctiveness from psychology."}, {"sourceSlug": "max-weber", "sourceName": "Max Weber", "verb": "SHAPES", "targetSlug": "sociology", "targetName": "Sociology", "context": "Weber's interpretive sociology, his concepts of bureaucracy, rationalisation, and social action, and his Protestant Ethic thesis are foundational to sociology's theoretical her